<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION  Road traffic accidents represent one of the most pressing global safety challenges of our era, claiming approximately 1.35 million lives annually worldwide. A significant proportion of these fatalities, estimated at around 90%, are attributed to human error and unsafe driving behaviours such as aggressive braking, sudden acceleration, sharp cornering, and erratic lane changes. Traditionally, identifying these dangerous driving patterns has relied on subjective human observation, costly physical monitoring systems, or post-accident analysis\204approaches that are neither scalable nor real-time. )/E(CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION  Road traffic accidents represent one of the most pressing global safety challenges of our era, claiming approximately 1.35 million lives annually worldwide. A significant proportion of these fatalities, estimated at around 90%, are attributed to human error and unsafe driving behaviours such as aggressive braking, sudden acceleration, sharp cornering, and erratic lane changes. Traditionally, identifying these dangerous driving patterns has relied on subjective human observation, costly physical monitoring systems, or post-accident analysis\204approaches that are neither scalable nor real-time. )/K[4]/P 171 0 R/Pg 8 0 R>>
endobj

176 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(This project addresses the critical gap in real-time, automated driving behaviour monitoring by developing a machine learning-based classifier that can accurately distinguish between three distinct driving behaviour categories: AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, and SLOW. The classifier operates on raw motion sensor data collected from smartphone accelerometers and gyroscopes\204 sensors that are universally present in modern mobile devices\204making this solution accessible and deployable without any additional hardware investment. )/E(This project addresses the critical gap in real-time, automated driving behaviour monitoring by developing a machine learning-based classifier that can accurately distinguish between three distinct driving behaviour categories: AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, and SLOW. The classifier operates on raw motion sensor data collected from smartphone accelerometers and gyroscopes\204 sensors that are universally present in modern mobile devices\204making this solution accessible and deployable without any additional hardware investment. )/K[5]/P 171 0 R/Pg 8 0 R>>
endobj

177 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The transportation domain is uniquely well-suited for machine learning applications. The availability of continuous, high-frequency sensor data, the well-defined classification boundaries between driving styles, and the direct safety implications make this an ideal problem for supervised classification algorithms. By leveraging the temporal and spatial characteristics of accelerometer \(AccX, AccY, AccZ\) and gyroscope \(GyroX, GyroY, GyroZ\) readings, the model learns to distinguish the smooth transitions of cautious driving from the abrupt force patterns that characterise aggressive manoeuvres. )/E(The transportation domain is uniquely well-suited for machine learning applications. The availability of continuous, high-frequency sensor data, the well-defined classification boundaries between driving styles, and the direct safety implications make this an ideal problem for supervised classification algorithms. By leveraging the temporal and spatial characteristics of accelerometer \(AccX, AccY, AccZ\) and gyroscope \(GyroX, GyroY, GyroZ\) readings, the model learns to distinguish the smooth transitions of cautious driving from the abrupt force patterns that characterise aggressive manoeuvres. )/K[6]/P 171 0 R/Pg 8 0 R>>
endobj

178 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The primary objective of this project is to build a classifier achieving greater than 90% accuracy with a prediction latency below 100 milliseconds, enabling real-time driver safety monitoring. Secondary applications include personalised insurance pricing based on actual driving behaviour, fleet management for commercial transport operators, and gamified driver coaching platforms.The successful deployment of such a system has the potential to materially reduce accident rates and save lives.  )/E(The primary objective of this project is to build a classifier achieving greater than 90% accuracy with a prediction latency below 100 milliseconds, enabling real-time driver safety monitoring. Secondary applications include personalised insurance pricing based on actual driving behaviour, fleet management for commercial transport operators, and gamified driver coaching platforms.The successful deployment of such a system has the potential to materially reduce accident rates and save lives.  )/K[7]/P 171 0 R/Pg 8 0 R>>
endobj

179 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/A[180 0 R 0]/K[8]/P 171 0 R/Pg 8 0 R>>
endobj

