327 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(CHAPTER 3: DATA ANALYSIS  3.1 Exploratory Data Analysis \(EDA\)  Exploratory Data Analysis was performed to understand the distribution of each feature, identify outliers, examine feature correlations, and visualise class-wise patterns. Key findings from the EDA phase are presented below. )/E(CHAPTER 3: DATA ANALYSIS  3.1 Exploratory Data Analysis \(EDA\)  Exploratory Data Analysis was performed to understand the distribution of each feature, identify outliers, examine feature correlations, and visualise class-wise patterns. Key findings from the EDA phase are presented below. )/K[5]/P 321 0 R/Pg 11 0 R>>
endobj

328 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(3.3 Feature Distributions by Class  Histograms were generated for all six sensor features, stratified by driving behaviour class. The plots reveal overlapping distributions across classes for raw sensor values, suggesting that derived features \(such as jerk and rolling statistics\) will be necessary to achieve high classification accuracy. The AGGRESSIVE class shows a noticeably wider spread in accelerometer readings, particularly in AccY and AccZ, reflecting the erratic nature of aggressive driving. )/E(3.3 Feature Distributions by Class  Histograms were generated for all six sensor features, stratified by driving behaviour class. The plots reveal overlapping distributions across classes for raw sensor values, suggesting that derived features \(such as jerk and rolling statistics\) will be necessary to achieve high classification accuracy. The AGGRESSIVE class shows a noticeably wider spread in accelerometer readings, particularly in AccY and AccZ, reflecting the erratic nature of aggressive driving. )/K[6]/P 321 0 R/Pg 11 0 R>>
endobj

329 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(3.2 Class Distribution Visualisation  The class distribution was examined using both a bar chart and a pie chart. The plots confirm that the dataset is approximately balanced, with SLOW being the most frequent class \(36.5%\) and AGGRESSIVE being the least frequent \(30.5%\). This near-uniform distribution is ideal for classification without resampling techniques. )/E(3.2 Class Distribution Visualisation  The class distribution was examined using both a bar chart and a pie chart. The plots confirm that the dataset is approximately balanced, with SLOW being the most frequent class \(36.5%\) and AGGRESSIVE being the least frequent \(30.5%\). This near-uniform distribution is ideal for classification without resampling techniques. )/K[7]/P 321 0 R/Pg 11 0 R>>
endobj

