767 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(4.6.3 Feature Importance  The Gradient Boosting model's feature importance scores reveal that rolling window statistics and jerk-based features dominate the top predictors. This confirms that temporal dynamics\204 specifically how acceleration changes over a 30-sample window\204are more informative than instantaneous sensor values alone. The top five features by importance are AccY_rolling_std \(0.2554\), JerkY_rolling_max \(0.1560\), JerkZ_rolling_max \(0.1320\), JerkX_rolling_max \(0.1309\), and AccX_rolling_std \(0.1208\).  The dominant importance of AccY_rolling_std \(0.2554\) reflects genuine physical significance  rather than a statistical artefact. AccY measures acceleration along the longitudinal axis of the  vehicle, which is the primary direction of braking and forward acceleration forces. The rolling )/E(4.6.3 Feature Importance  The Gradient Boosting model's feature importance scores reveal that rolling window statistics and jerk-based features dominate the top predictors. This confirms that temporal dynamics\204 specifically how acceleration changes over a 30-sample window\204are more informative than instantaneous sensor values alone. The top five features by importance are AccY_rolling_std \(0.2554\), JerkY_rolling_max \(0.1560\), JerkZ_rolling_max \(0.1320\), JerkX_rolling_max \(0.1309\), and AccX_rolling_std \(0.1208\).  The dominant importance of AccY_rolling_std \(0.2554\) reflects genuine physical significance  rather than a statistical artefact. AccY measures acceleration along the longitudinal axis of the  vehicle, which is the primary direction of braking and forward acceleration forces. The rolling )/K[4]/P 763 0 R/Pg 21 0 R>>
endobj

768 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(standard deviation of this signal over a 30-sample window directly quantifies how erratically a driver modulates these forces over time\204high variability corresponds to frequent, sharp accelerations and hard braking that are the hallmark signatures of aggressive driving, while low variability characterises the smooth, gradual inputs of cautious or normal driving. This physical grounding is further supported by the model\220s generalisation performance: the train-test F1 gap of just 0.0041 and the near-zero cross-validation variance confirm that the model has not simply memorised training-set noise. A feature that was merely an overfitting artefact would not maintain predictive power across unseen data folds; the consistency of AccY_rolling_std\220s contribution across all cross-validation splits therefore confirms that its high importance score is a true reflection of its discriminative value for driving behaviour classification. )/E(standard deviation of this signal over a 30-sample window directly quantifies how erratically a driver modulates these forces over time\204high variability corresponds to frequent, sharp accelerations and hard braking that are the hallmark signatures of aggressive driving, while low variability characterises the smooth, gradual inputs of cautious or normal driving. This physical grounding is further supported by the model\220s generalisation performance: the train-test F1 gap of just 0.0041 and the near-zero cross-validation variance confirm that the model has not simply memorised training-set noise. A feature that was merely an overfitting artefact would not maintain predictive power across unseen data folds; the consistency of AccY_rolling_std\220s contribution across all cross-validation splits therefore confirms that its high importance score is a true reflection of its discriminative value for driving behaviour classification. )/K[5]/P 763 0 R/Pg 21 0 R>>
endobj

