875 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(CHAPTER 5: CONCLUSION AND FUTURE SCOPE  5.1 Conclusion  This project successfully developed a machine learning pipeline for classifying driving behaviour into three categories\204AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, and SLOW\204from smartphone accelerometer and gyroscope data. The end-to-end pipeline encompassed data quality validation, exploratory data analysis, feature engineering, model training, hyperparameter tuning, and rigorous evaluation. )/E(CHAPTER 5: CONCLUSION AND FUTURE SCOPE  5.1 Conclusion  This project successfully developed a machine learning pipeline for classifying driving behaviour into three categories\204AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, and SLOW\204from smartphone accelerometer and gyroscope data. The end-to-end pipeline encompassed data quality validation, exploratory data analysis, feature engineering, model training, hyperparameter tuning, and rigorous evaluation. )/K[4]/P 871 0 R/Pg 24 0 R>>
endobj

876 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The dataset was well-suited to this task: 3,644 balanced samples with zero missing values and clear temporal structure enabled robust training and evaluation. The multi-model comparison clearly demonstrated the superiority of ensemble tree-based methods \(Gradient Boosting and Random Forest\) over linear classifiers and probabilistic models for this non-linear classification task. )/E(The dataset was well-suited to this task: 3,644 balanced samples with zero missing values and clear temporal structure enabled robust training and evaluation. The multi-model comparison clearly demonstrated the superiority of ensemble tree-based methods \(Gradient Boosting and Random Forest\) over linear classifiers and probabilistic models for this non-linear classification task. )/K[5]/P 871 0 R/Pg 24 0 R>>
endobj

877 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The Gradient Boosting Classifier emerged as the best-performing model with a test accuracy of 99.18% and a weighted F1-Score of 0.9917, significantly surpassing the project's target of 90% accuracy. The model demonstrated exceptional class-specific performance, achieving perfect recall for NORMAL driving and near-perfect precision for AGGRESSIVE driving. The near-zero train-test gap confirms that the model generalises well to unseen data. )/E(The Gradient Boosting Classifier emerged as the best-performing model with a test accuracy of 99.18% and a weighted F1-Score of 0.9917, significantly surpassing the project's target of 90% accuracy. The model demonstrated exceptional class-specific performance, achieving perfect recall for NORMAL driving and near-perfect precision for AGGRESSIVE driving. The near-zero train-test gap confirms that the model generalises well to unseen data. )/K[6]/P 871 0 R/Pg 24 0 R>>
endobj

878 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(A key finding of this project is the central role of jerk-based features and rolling window statistics in differentiating driving behaviours. The top five features by importance\204AccY_rolling_std, JerkY_rolling_max, JerkZ_rolling_max, JerkX_rolling_max, and AccX_rolling_std\204all belong to the engineered feature set, demonstrating the value of temporal feature extraction from raw sensor streams. Raw sensor readings alone \(as captured by Logistic Regression with only 59.3% accuracy\) are insufficient for reliable driving behaviour classification. )/E(A key finding of this project is the central role of jerk-based features and rolling window statistics in differentiating driving behaviours. The top five features by importance\204AccY_rolling_std, JerkY_rolling_max, JerkZ_rolling_max, JerkX_rolling_max, and AccX_rolling_std\204all belong to the engineered feature set, demonstrating the value of temporal feature extraction from raw sensor streams. Raw sensor readings alone \(as captured by Logistic Regression with only 59.3% accuracy\) are insufficient for reliable driving behaviour classification. )/K[7]/P 871 0 R/Pg 24 0 R>>
endobj

