985 0 obj
<</Title(CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION  Road traffic accidents represent one of the most pressing global safety challenges of our era, claiming approximately 1.35 million lives annually worldwide. A significant proportion of these fatalities, estimated at around 90%, are attributed to human error and unsafe driving behaviours such as aggressive braking, sudden acceleration, sharp cornering, and erratic lane changes. Traditionally, identifying these dangerous driving patterns has relied on subjective human observation, costly physical monitoring systems, or post-accident analysis\204approaches that are neither scalable nor real-time.)/Dest[8 0 R/Fit]/Parent 984 0 R/Next 986 0 R>>
endobj

986 0 obj
<</Title(This project addresses the critical gap in real-time, automated driving behaviour monitoring by developing a machine learning-based classifier that can accurately distinguish between three distinct driving behaviour categories: AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, and SLOW. The classifier operates on raw motion sensor data collected from smartphone accelerometers and gyroscopes\204 sensors that are universally present in modern mobile devices\204making this solution accessible and deployable without any additional hardware investment.)/Dest[8 0 R/Fit]/Parent 984 0 R/Prev 985 0 R/Next 987 0 R>>
endobj

987 0 obj
<</Title(The transportation domain is uniquely well-suited for machine learning applications. The availability of continuous, high-frequency sensor data, the well-defined classification boundaries between driving styles, and the direct safety implications make this an ideal problem for supervised classification algorithms. By leveraging the temporal and spatial characteristics of accelerometer \(AccX, AccY, AccZ\) and gyroscope \(GyroX, GyroY, GyroZ\) readings, the model learns to distinguish the smooth transitions of cautious driving from the abrupt force patterns that characterise aggressive manoeuvres.)/Dest[8 0 R/Fit]/Parent 984 0 R/Prev 986 0 R/Next 988 0 R>>
endobj

988 0 obj
<</Title(The primary objective of this project is to build a classifier achieving greater than 90% accuracy with a prediction latency below 100 milliseconds, enabling real-time driver safety monitoring. Secondary applications include personalised insurance pricing based on actual driving behaviour, fleet management for commercial transport operators, and gamified driver coaching platforms.The successful deployment of such a system has the potential to materially reduce accident rates and save lives.)/Dest[8 0 R/Fit]/Parent 984 0 R/Prev 987 0 R/Next 989 0 R>>
endobj

989 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 984 0 R/Prev 988 0 R>>
endobj

990 0 obj
<</Title(CHAPTER 2: DATA DESCRIPTION)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Count -2/First 991 0 R/Last 992 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 984 0 R/Next 993 0 R>>
endobj

991 0 obj
<</Title(2.1 Dataset Overview  The dataset used in this project is the Motion Sensor Driving Behaviour Dataset \(train_motion_data.csv\), which contains time-series readings from smartphone-based inertial measurement units \(IMUs\) recorded during real driving sessions. The dataset captures sensor signals from three categories of driving behaviour: AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, and SLOW.)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 990 0 R/Next 992 0 R>>
endobj

992 0 obj
<</Title(2.2 Feature Description  The raw dataset contains six sensor features derived from smartphone motion sensors, alongside a class label and a timestamp. The features are described below:)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 990 0 R/Prev 991 0 R>>
endobj

