<</Title(Nature of Behaviour)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 997 0 R/Prev 999 0 R>>
endobj

1001 0 obj
<</Title(2.4 Dataset Suitability Justification  This dataset is particularly suitable for the proposed classification task for the following reasons:)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 993 0 R/Prev 994 0 R/Next 1002 0 R>>
endobj

1002 0 obj
<</Title(The data originates from real smartphone sensors\204the same hardware available in any consumer device\204making the trained model directly applicable in production.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 993 0 R/Prev 1001 0 R/Next 1003 0 R>>
endobj

1003 0 obj
<</Title(The three-class structure aligns perfectly with the problem statement of distinguishing aggressive, normal, and slow driving.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 993 0 R/Prev 1002 0 R/Next 1004 0 R>>
endobj

1004 0 obj
<</Title(The dataset contains no missing values and no duplicate rows, indicating high data quality with minimal preprocessing overhead.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 993 0 R/Prev 1003 0 R/Next 1005 0 R>>
endobj

1005 0 obj
<</Title(The balanced class distribution \(30\20537% per class\) ensures that the classifier is not biased towards any single behaviour category.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 993 0 R/Prev 1004 0 R/Next 1006 0 R>>
endobj

1006 0 obj
<</Title(The temporal ordering of records \(via timestamps\) permits the extraction of time-domain features such as rolling statistics and jerk calculations, enriching the feature space significantly.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 993 0 R/Prev 1005 0 R>>
endobj

1007 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Count -3/First 1008 0 R/Last 1010 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 993 0 R/Next 1011 0 R>>
endobj

1008 0 obj
<</Title(CHAPTER 3: DATA ANALYSIS  3.1 Exploratory Data Analysis \(EDA\)  Exploratory Data Analysis was performed to understand the distribution of each feature, identify outliers, examine feature correlations, and visualise class-wise patterns. Key findings from the EDA phase are presented below.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1007 0 R/Next 1009 0 R>>
endobj

1009 0 obj
<</Title(3.2 Class Distribution Visualisation  The class distribution was examined using both a bar chart and a pie chart. The plots confirm that the dataset is approximately balanced, with SLOW being the most frequent class \(36.5%\) and AGGRESSIVE being the least frequent \(30.5%\). This near-uniform distribution is ideal for classification without resampling techniques.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1007 0 R/Prev 1008 0 R/Next 1010 0 R>>
endobj

1010 0 obj
<</Title(3.3 Feature Distributions by Class  Histograms were generated for all six sensor features, stratified by driving behaviour class. The plots reveal overlapping distributions across classes for raw sensor values, suggesting that derived features \(such as jerk and rolling statistics\) will be necessary to achieve high classification accuracy. The AGGRESSIVE class shows a noticeably wider spread in accelerometer readings, particularly in AccY and AccZ, reflecting the erratic nature of aggressive driving.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1007 0 R/Prev 1009 0 R>>
endobj

1011 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1012 0 R/Last 1012 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1007 0 R/Next 1013 0 R>>
endobj

1012 0 obj
<</Title(3.4 Box Plot Analysis  Box plots were constructed to visualise the inter-quartile ranges and outliers for each feature across the three driving classes. The plots confirm that the AGGRESSIVE class exhibits greater variability \(wider IQR\) in AccX, AccY, and AccZ compared to NORMAL and SLOW classes, providing early evidence that acceleration magnitude and variability are discriminative features.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1011 0 R>>
endobj

1013 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1014 0 R/Last 1014 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1011 0 R/Next 1015 0 R>>
endobj

1014 0 obj
<</Title(3.5 Correlation Analysis  A Pearson correlation matrix was computed for the six sensor features. The analysis reveals that features are largely uncorrelated with each other \(most correlations below 0.3\), with the strongest correlation observed between AccX and GyroZ \(\2120.32\). This low inter-feature correlation is beneficial, as it indicates that each sensor axis provides unique information, and multicollinearity is not a concern for tree-based models.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1013 0 R>>
endobj

1015 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1016 0 R/Last 1016 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1013 0 R/Next 1017 0 R>>
endobj

1016 0 obj
<</Title(3.6 Time-Series Pattern Analysis  A sample of 200 consecutive records from each class was plotted as a time series to observe the temporal characteristics of each driving style. The plots clearly demonstrate that AGGRESSIVE driving produces high-amplitude, erratic oscillations in acceleration, while SLOW driving shows a noticeably wider spread in accelerometer readings, particularly in AccY and AccZ, reflecting the erratic nature of aggressive driving.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1015 0 R>>
endobj

1017 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1015 0 R/Next 1018 0 R>>
endobj

1018 0 obj
<</Title(3.7 Outlier Analysis)/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1019 0 R/Last 1019 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1017 0 R/Next 1020 0 R>>
endobj

1019 0 obj
<</Title(An IQR-based outlier detection was performed on all sensor features. Outliers were identified at varying rates across features, with GyroZ exhibiting the highest outlier percentage \(10.68%\) and AccX the lowest \(3.07%\). Rather than removing outliers, they were retained in the dataset as they may represent genuine extreme driving events \(e.g., emergency braking or sharp cornering\) that are important for classifying aggressive behaviour.)/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1018 0 R>>
endobj

1020 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Count -5/First 1021 0 R/Last 1025 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1018 0 R/Next 1026 0 R>>
endobj

1021 0 obj
<</Title(CHAPTER 4: DATA MODELLING  4.1 Feature Engineering  Feature engineering is a critical step that transforms raw sensor readings into a richer representation that enables machine learning models to detect subtle patterns. A total of 32 new features were engineered from the original 6 sensor measurements, bringing the total feature count to 38. The following categories of features were created:)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1020 0 R/Next 1022 0 R>>
endobj

1022 0 obj
<</Title<>/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1020 0 R/Prev 1021 0 R/Next 1023 0 R>>
endobj

1023 0 obj
<</Title(Magnitude Features \(3 features\): Three magnitude features were computed as the Euclidean norm across axes: AccMagnitude, GyroMagnitude, and JerkMagnitude. These scalar measures capture the overall intensity of motion irrespective of direction.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1020 0 R/Prev 1022 0 R/Next 1024 0 R>>
endobj

1024 0 obj
<</Title(Rolling Window Statistics \(12 features\): A 30-sample sliding window was applied to compute rolling means and rolling standard deviations for accelerometer readings, and rolling means and rolling maxima for jerk values. These features capture the local temporal dynamics of each driving segment.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1020 0 R/Prev 1023 0 R/Next 1025 0 R>>
endobj

1025 0 obj
<</Title(Statistical Aggregation Features \(7 features\): Additional features including squared accelerations \(AccX_var, AccY_var, AccZ_var\), absolute accelerations \(AbsAccX, AbsAccY, AbsAccZ\), and Acc_Energy were computed to capture the energycontent of the sensor signals.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1020 0 R/Prev 1024 0 R>>
endobj

1026 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Count -9/First 1027 0 R/Last 1035 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1020 0 R/Next 1036 0 R>>
endobj

1027 0 obj
<</Title(4.2 Reason to Select Machine Learning Models  Eight supervised classification algorithms were selected for this study. The rationale for each is as follows:)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Next 1028 0 R>>
endobj

1028 0 obj
<</Title(Gradient Boosting Classifier: Selected as the primary candidate due to its sequential ensemble approach that corrects errors of previous learners, making it highly effective for datasets with complex non-linear decision boundaries.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1027 0 R/Next 1029 0 R>>
endobj

1029 0 obj
<</Title(Random Forest Classifier: An ensemble of decision trees that reduces variance through bootstrap aggregation, providing both high accuracy and built-in feature importance ranking.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1028 0 R/Next 1030 0 R>>
endobj

1030 0 obj
<</Title(Decision Tree Classifier: A simple, interpretable baseline that provides insight into the decision logic, useful for understanding feature thresholds that separate classes.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1029 0 R/Next 1031 0 R>>
endobj

1031 0 obj
<</Title(Support Vector Machine \(SVM\): Effective in high-dimensional spaces; the RBF kernel was employed to model non-linear class boundaries.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1030 0 R/Next 1032 0 R>>
endobj

1032 0 obj
<</Title(K-Nearest Neighbours \(KNN\): A non-parametric instance-based method that classifies samples based on proximity to training examples in feature space.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1031 0 R/Next 1033 0 R>>
endobj

1033 0 obj
<</Title(Logistic Regression: A linear baseline model that provides a lower-bound performance estimate and offers high interpretability.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1032 0 R/Next 1034 0 R>>
endobj

1034 0 obj
<</Title(Gaussian Naive Bayes: A probabilistic model based on Bayes' theorem with strong independence assumptions, included for completeness.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1033 0 R/Next 1035 0 R>>
endobj

1035 0 obj
<</Title(AdaBoost Classifier: An adaptive boosting method that combines multiple weak learners, useful for comparison with other ensemble approaches.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1026 0 R/Prev 1034 0 R>>
endobj

1036 0 obj
<</Title(4.3 Algorithm: Gradient Boosting Classifier)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Count -2/First 1037 0 R/Last 1038 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1026 0 R/Next 1039 0 R>>
endobj

1037 0 obj
<</Title(Gradient Boosting is an ensemble machine learning technique that constructs models sequentially, where each subsequent model corrects the errors of its predecessor. The algorithm minimises a differentiable loss function using gradient descent in function space.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1036 0 R/Next 1038 0 R>>
endobj

1038 0 obj
<</Title<>/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1036 0 R/Prev 1037 0 R>>
endobj

1039 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1036 0 R/Next 1040 0 R>>
endobj

1040 0 obj
<</Title(4.4 Data Preparation)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Count -4/First 1041 0 R/Last 1044 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1039 0 R/Next 1045 0 R>>
endobj

1041 0 obj
<</Title(The data preparation pipeline consisted of the following steps:)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1040 0 R/Next 1042 0 R>>
endobj

1042 0 obj
<</Title(Label Encoding: The categorical target variable \(AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, SLOW\) was encoded numerically as 0, 1, and 2 respectively using scikit-learn's LabelEncoder.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1040 0 R/Prev 1041 0 R/Next 1043 0 R>>
endobj

1043 0 obj
<</Title(Train-Test Split: The dataset was partitioned into a training set \(80%, n = 2,915\) and a test set \(20%, n = 729\) using stratified sampling to preserve class proportions.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1040 0 R/Prev 1042 0 R/Next 1044 0 R>>
endobj

1044 0 obj
<</Title(Feature Scaling: All 38 features were standardised using StandardScaler, resulting in zero mean and unit variance. This ensures that distance-based algorithms \(SVM, KNN\) are not dominated by features with larger numerical ranges.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1040 0 R/Prev 1043 0 R>>
endobj

1045 0 obj
<</Title(4.5 Model Performance Comparison)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1046 0 R/Last 1046 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1040 0 R/Next 1047 0 R>>
endobj

1046 0 obj
<</Title(All eight models were trained on the standardised training set and evaluated on the held-out test set. The performance metrics \(Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1-Score\) are summarised below:)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1045 0 R>>
endobj

1047 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[19 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1048 0 R/Last 1048 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1045 0 R/Next 1049 0 R>>
endobj

1048 0 obj
<</Title(4.6 Result Analysis  4.6.1 Best Model: Gradient Boosting Classifier  The Gradient Boosting Classifier achieved the highest performance across all metrics, with a test accuracy of 99.18% and a weighted F1-Score of 0.9917. The detailed per-class classification report demonstrates exceptional precision and recall across all three driving behaviour categories:)/Dest[19 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1047 0 R>>
endobj

1049 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[20 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1050 0 R/Last 1050 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1047 0 R/Next 1051 0 R>>
endobj

1050 0 obj
<</Title(4.6.2 Confusion Matrix  The confusion matrix for the Gradient Boosting Classifier demonstrates near-perfect classification with only 6 misclassified samples out of 729 test instances. NORMAL driving was classified with 100% recall \(zero false negatives\), indicating that the model never incorrectly categorises normal driving behaviour.)/Dest[20 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1049 0 R>>
endobj

1051 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[21 0 R/Fit]/Count -2/First 1052 0 R/Last 1053 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1049 0 R/Next 1054 0 R>>
endobj

1052 0 obj
<</Title(4.6.3 Feature Importance  The Gradient Boosting model's feature importance scores reveal that rolling window statistics and jerk-based features dominate the top predictors. This confirms that temporal dynamics\204 specifically how acceleration changes over a 30-sample window\204are more informative than instantaneous sensor values alone. The top five features by importance are AccY_rolling_std \(0.2554\), JerkY_rolling_max \(0.1560\), JerkZ_rolling_max \(0.1320\), JerkX_rolling_max \(0.1309\), and AccX_rolling_std \(0.1208\).  The dominant importance of AccY_rolling_std \(0.2554\) reflects genuine physical significance  rather than a statistical artefact. AccY measures acceleration along the longitudinal axis of the  vehicle, which is the primary direction of braking and forward acceleration forces. The rolling)/Dest[21 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1051 0 R/Next 1053 0 R>>
endobj

1053 0 obj
<</Title(standard deviation of this signal over a 30-sample window directly quantifies how erratically a driver modulates these forces over time\204high variability corresponds to frequent, sharp accelerations and hard braking that are the hallmark signatures of aggressive driving, while low variability characterises the smooth, gradual inputs of cautious or normal driving. This physical grounding is further supported by the model\220s generalisation performance: the train-test F1 gap of just 0.0041 and the near-zero cross-validation variance confirm that the model has not simply memorised training-set noise. A feature that was merely an overfitting artefact would not maintain predictive power across unseen data folds; the consistency of AccY_rolling_std\220s contribution across all cross-validation splits therefore confirms that its high importance score is a true reflection of its discriminative value for driving behaviour classification.)/Dest[21 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1051 0 R/Prev 1052 0 R>>
endobj

1054 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[22 0 R/Fit]/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1051 0 R/Next 1055 0 R>>
endobj

1055 0 obj
<</Title(4.6.4 Cross-Validation)/Dest[22 0 R/Fit]/Count -1/First 1056 0 R/Last 1056 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1054 0 R/Next 1057 0 R>>
endobj

1056 0 obj
<</Title(A 5-fold stratified cross-validation was performed on the training set to assess model generalisation. The results are presented below:)/Dest[22 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1055 0 R>>
endobj

1057 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[23 0 R/Fit]/Count -2/First 1058 0 R/Last 1059 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1055 0 R/Next 1060 0 R>>
endobj

1058 0 obj
<</Title(4.6.5 Overfitting Analysis  A systematic overfitting check was conducted by comparing training performance, test performance, and cross-validation scores. The train-test F1-Score gap of 0.0041 and the CV-test gap of \2120.0031 both indicate that the model generalises extremely well without overfitting to the training data.)/Dest[23 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1057 0 R/Next 1059 0 R>>
endobj

1059 0 obj
<</Title(4.7 Hyperparameter Tuning Results  GridSearchCV with 3-fold cross-validation was applied over 18 parameter combinations. The optimal hyperparameters identified were learning_rate = 0.15, max_depth = 7, and n_estimators = 150. The tuned model achieved a test accuracy of 0.9959 and an F1-Score of 0.9959, representing  a marginal improvement of 0.0041 over the baseline configuration.)/Dest[23 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1057 0 R/Prev 1058 0 R>>
endobj

1060 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Count -4/First 1061 0 R/Last 1064 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1057 0 R/Next 1065 0 R>>
endobj

1061 0 obj
<</Title(CHAPTER 5: CONCLUSION AND FUTURE SCOPE  5.1 Conclusion  This project successfully developed a machine learning pipeline for classifying driving behaviour into three categories\204AGGRESSIVE, NORMAL, and SLOW\204from smartphone accelerometer and gyroscope data. The end-to-end pipeline encompassed data quality validation, exploratory data analysis, feature engineering, model training, hyperparameter tuning, and rigorous evaluation.)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1060 0 R/Next 1062 0 R>>
endobj

1062 0 obj
<</Title(The Gradient Boosting Classifier emerged as the best-performing model with a test accuracy of 99.18% and a weighted F1-Score of 0.9917, significantly surpassing the project's target of 90% accuracy. The model demonstrated exceptional class-specific performance, achieving perfect recall for NORMAL driving and near-perfect precision for AGGRESSIVE driving. The near-zero train-test gap confirms that the model generalises well to unseen data.)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1060 0 R/Prev 1061 0 R/Next 1063 0 R>>
endobj

1063 0 obj
<</Title(A key finding of this project is the central role of jerk-based features and rolling window statistics in differentiating driving behaviours. The top five features by importance\204AccY_rolling_std, JerkY_rolling_max, JerkZ_rolling_max, JerkX_rolling_max, and AccX_rolling_std\204all belong to the engineered feature set, demonstrating the value of temporal feature extraction from raw sensor streams. Raw sensor readings alone \(as captured by Logistic Regression with only 59.3% accuracy\) are insufficient for reliable driving behaviour classification.)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1060 0 R/Prev 1062 0 R/Next 1064 0 R>>
endobj

1064 0 obj
<</Title(The dataset was well-suited to this task: 3,644 balanced samples with zero missing values and clear temporal structure enabled robust training and evaluation. The multi-model comparison clearly demonstrated the superiority of ensemble tree-based methods \(Gradient Boosting and Random Forest\) over linear classifiers and probabilistic models for this non-linear classification task.)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1060 0 R/Prev 1063 0 R>>
endobj

1065 0 obj
<</Title(Department of Computer Science and Engineering \(Data Science\))/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Count -9/First 1066 0 R/Last 1074 0 R/Parent 6497 0 R/Prev 1060 0 R/Next 1075 0 R>>
endobj

1066 0 obj
<</Title(5.2 Future Scope  While the results of this study are highly promising, several avenues exist for further development:)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Next 1067 0 R>>
endobj

1067 0 obj
<</Title(Deep Learning for Temporal Modelling: Long Short-Term Memory \(LSTM\) networks and Transformer-based architectures could be explored to capture long-range temporal dependencies in sensor data that window-based statistics may miss.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1066 0 R/Next 1068 0 R>>
endobj

1068 0 obj
<</Title(Real-Time Deployment: The model can be integrated into a mobile application or an embedded system \(e.g., Raspberry Pi\) for real-time driving behaviour monitoring, alerting drivers when aggressive behaviour is detected.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1067 0 R/Next 1069 0 R>>
endobj

1069 0 obj
<</Title(Multi-Modal Data Fusion: Incorporating GPS data \(speed, heading, route type\), road condition information, and traffic density could further improve classification accuracy and contextual understanding.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1068 0 R/Next 1070 0 R>>
endobj

1070 0 obj
<</Title(Personalised Driver Models: Different drivers have different baseline behaviours. Driver-specific models trained on individual driving history could reduce false alarms and improve the precision of aggressive driving detection.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1069 0 R/Next 1071 0 R>>
endobj

1071 0 obj
<</Title(Insurance Telematics Integration: The classifier can be integrated into Usage-Based Insurance \(UBI\) platforms to compute personalised risk scores, rewarding safe drivers with lower premiums.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1070 0 R/Next 1072 0 R>>
endobj

1072 0 obj
<</Title(Fleet Management Dashboards: For commercial transport operators, a fleet-wide monitoring dashboard could aggregate driver behaviour scores, enabling targeted driver training and proactive maintenance scheduling.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1071 0 R/Next 1073 0 R>>
endobj

1073 0 obj
<</Title(Federated Learning: To address data privacy concerns, federated learning approaches could allow model training across distributed devices without transmitting raw sensor data to a central server.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1072 0 R/Next 1074 0 R>>
endobj

1074 0 obj
<</Title(Larger and More Diverse Datasets: Collecting data across different vehicle types, road types, weather conditions, and geographic regions would improve the model's robustness and generalisability.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1065 0 R/Prev 1073 0 R>>
endobj

